Please identify the correct one sentence summary of the book The Fall of Hyperion. Please respond with only "A", "B", "C", or "D".
(A) The Fall of Hyperion is the second novel in Dan Simmons' Hyperion Cantos series, continuing the story of the pilgrims on their journey to confront the enigmatic and powerful being known as the Shrike, amidst a universe on the brink of interstellar war. 
(B) "The Fall of Hyperion" is the second novel in Dan Simmons' Hyperion Cantos, continuing the saga begun in "Hyperion." Set in a distant future where humanity has spread across the stars, the novel delves deeper into the mysteries of the enigmatic Time Tombs on the planet Hyperion and the Shrike, the feared and worshiped creature that resides within them. The story follows a diverse group of pilgrims who have been granted the rare opportunity to make a pilgrimage to the Time Tombs, each with their own reasons and personal stories tied to the Shrike. As the pilgrims journey, they are caught in the crossfire of a galactic conflict between the Hegemony of Man and the Ousters, nomadic humans who have adapted to life in the void. The novel intricately weaves together themes of religion, science, and human nature, exploring the consequences of technological advancement and the quest for immortality. The narrative is rich with literary allusions and complex character development, pushing the boundaries of science fiction and inviting readers to ponder the nature of time, faith, and the human condition.
(C) Breakfast of Champions is a satirical novel by Kurt Vonnegut that follows the intersecting lives of various characters leading up to a bizarre science fiction convention, where the author himself becomes a character, questioning the nature of free will and the absurdity of human existence.
(D) "Breakfast of Champions" is a satirical novel by Kurt Vonnegut, published in 1973, which blends elements of science fiction, social commentary, and dark humor. The story follows two main characters: Kilgore Trout, a struggling science fiction writer, and Dwayne Hoover, a Pontiac dealer in Midland City, Ohio. As Dwayne becomes increasingly unhinged due to personal and professional crises, he encounters Kilgore at a local event, the annual Midland City Arts Festival. Trout's writings, which often blur the lines between reality and fiction, lead Dwayne to a mental breakdown, believing he has been chosen to save the world. Vonnegut interjects throughout the narrative with his own voice, commenting on the absurdity of human behavior and the arbitrary nature of life. The novel is a critique of American culture, capitalism, and the human condition, using its quirky characters and surreal plot to explore themes of free will, destiny, and the search for meaning in a seemingly indifferent universe.
